ke but you know i  i think this is not just about britain i  think this is this is the key you know  we are a military we have a military  power we saw what happened in 2001  and people forget in 2001 there were two  military operations there was the  operation that went in to take out  al-qaeda  and remove parts of the taliban from  power because of 9 11. and that was done  pretty swiftly and pretty quickly and  then of course there was the nato  mission and that the follow-on mission  which was about stabilization and  international development at the first  authorized operations was a success and  ultimately ended in the death of osama  bin laden um but i think i think you  know and we'll retain that but i think  the lesson here is also it's not just  about britain it's really easy for for  people to make the narrative of britain  has let down afghanistan if you're going  to go to afghanistan it was very very  clear that you had to go as an  international community and the reason  you have to do that is it's not just  about military power  it's about political power it's about  soft power  you know i i've seen a number of people  saying oh britain can't do it we could  send 10 000 troops tomorrow morning and  we could we could deploy them for a  couple of years probably in afghanistan  but if you're on your own if you're on  your own you you won't get the political  leverage of the neighboring countries  that you need you won't be able to  effectively defend your position by  saying to the taliban or whoever it else  is this is the international community  concerned  and there are lots of things to be done  to put it right if you are purely a  country on your own the soviet union for  example you then face a different  consequence okay so while that diplomacy  i'm sure will be reignited with the  united states given what we're seeing  hour by unfolding in afghanistan  meantime for now we have left behind a  humanitarian crisis uh general nick  carter chief of the